by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-10T17:17:09Z

Applied the review and reworked the extractor integration.

The attribute path is now a dedicated branch: `getType()` dispatches directly on `$accessors->adder`/`setter`/`getter`, and `getAccessorMethod()`/`getMutatorMethod()` are back to their original discovery-only shape. `isWritable()` gets the same early return `getReadInfo()`/`getWriteInfo()` already had.

That removes the three `array_first()` calls, and with them the `symfony/polyfill-php85` requirement, so `composer.json` is untouched now. They existed only to invent a prefix for a `[$method, $prefix]` contract meant for discovery, and inventing it was not harmless:

```php
#[WithAccessors(getter: 'isEnabled')]
private ?string $enabled = null;
public function isEnabled(): bool {}
```

`getType()` returned `bool` with the default prefixes but `null|string` with `accessorPrefixes: ['is', 'get']`. The fabricated prefix came from `accessorPrefixes` and was then tested against `array_diff($accessorPrefixes, ['is','can','has'])`, a different set, so an explicitly named getter still depended on prefix ordering. Covered by `testAttributeAccessorWinsWhateverThePrefixesAre`.

The inflector no longer arbitrates either. `Bar::$tags` and `Bar::$mailbox` declare the same four accessors, yet `tags` resolved to `array` and `mailbox` to `list<int>`, purely because `singularize()` treats "mailbox" as already singular. The adder now wins whenever it is named, so both give `list<int>`; the expectation for `tags` moved accordingly.

I did not add the rule rejecting `setter` combined with `adder`. `Bar` uses that combination deliberately and it mirrors what PropertyAccess supports natively (`setTags()` alongside `addTag()`/`removeTag()`), so forbidding it would drop a real case rather than tighten one.

Also applied: both exception message suggestions, and the attribute is no longer iterated as an object, so a future public property cannot silently become a method name.

Verified: PropertyInfo 613 tests, PropertyAccess 487, Serializer 1426, Form 4904, Validator 5124, no failures. Reverting the extractor while keeping the new tests fails them.

One more thing while rebasing: the CHANGELOG line was in the released `8.1` section although this targets 8.2, so it moved to a new `8.2` section.
